  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nLight rain showers continue to drift through part of the area this \nafternoon. The abundant cloud cover has keep high temperatures on \nthe cooler side with highs only warming to 60s and 70s. Rainfall \namounts up to a few hundredths to a tenth or so remain mainly across \nsouth central South Dakota. Any chance for rain will come to an end \nby this evening, leaving partly cloudy to cloudy skies for the \novernight hours. Despite the cloud cover, a surface high pushing \nthrough the area will keep light east/southeast flow going through \nthe overnight timeframe, allowing for low temperatures to fall to \nthe 50s to low 60s across the area.\n\nCloudy skies will persist through the day tomorrow, keeping high \ntemperatures near to below average again in the 70s with a few spots \nreaching into the low 80s. Southerly return flow will pick up as the \npreviously mentioned surface high departs to the southeast. Winds \nwill still remain on the lighter side but a few gusts up to 20-25 \nmph is expected. Another shortwave trough will push through the \nforecast area, resulting in showers across the area once again. \nThese showers look to be more widespread though remaining along and \nsouth of I-90 for the day. Forcing will be a bit stronger with this \nchance for rain along with very minor instability being present at \nor below 100 J/kg. This will bring overall higher rainfall amounts \nto locations along and south of a Chamberlain, South Dakota to \nBeresford, South Dakota to Storm Lake, Iowa where rainfall amounts \nbetween a quarter to a half an inch are possible (20-60%). Lighter \namounts are expected north of this line. With the very minor \ninstability in play, an isolated rumble of thunder is possible. This \nlight rain will persist into the night before pushing southeast of \nthe area by Friday morning.\n\nUpper level ridging will begin to build over the central Plains on \nFriday, raising high temperatures back to the upper 70s, 80s, and \nlow 90s. This trend will continue through the weekend as the thermal \nridge slides eastwards. High temperatures look to return to above \naverage with highs in the 90s to low 100s. The ensembles support \nthis as they show high probabilities (60-100% chance) for high \ntemperatures to exceed 90F along and west of I-90 over the weekend. \nTheir probabilities drop to a 30-40% chance for highs to break 100F \nover the same area. The EFT also supports this as it shows \n60-100% of its members suggesting anomalously warm temperatures \nthis weekend and a shift of tails exceeding 1. Thus, will have \nto continue to monitor the potential for heat headlines being \nneeded, especially as dew points look to remain in the 60s and \n70s.\n\nThe heat looks to begin to wane on Monday as high temperatures look \nto only warm to the 90s. Highs will slightly cool into the middle of \nnext week. With the forecast area being just on the northern \ngradient of the upper level ridge, ridge riding shortwaves could \nbring rain and perhaps storm chances back to the area. The ensembles \nonly show low probabilities ranging from 0 to 30% so confidence is \nlow in this potential as of now. Machine learning guidance also only \nshows a half a percent to about a 5% chance for severe storms Sunday \nthrough the middle of next week. Something to keep an eye on as we \nhead into another hot and humid pattern.",
